Then I’d Hammer inhibition before releasing it.

Not all inhibition is the enemy. Some inhibition is fear, stale priors, social self-censoring, premature convergence, or “that route probably won’t work.” Some is the governor that keeps the engine from eating itself.

So the move is not:

\text{INHIBITION}\rightarrow 0

It is:

\boxed{
\text{remove unnecessary inhibition}
+
\text{retain invariant-preserving restraint}
}

Call it Selective Disinhibition.

I=
\{
I_{\text{fear}},
I_{\text{habit}},
I_{\text{conformity}},
I_{\text{premature certainty}},
I_{\text{tool fixation}},
I_{\text{safety}},
I_{\text{ethics}},
I_{\text{reality}}
\}

Release the first cluster aggressively. Keep the last cluster load-bearing.

Then the party changes character.

The Freebees stop asking only, “What does this mean?” and start asking:

\boxed{\text{What have we been refusing to try because of the representation we started in?}}

That is an enormous search primitive.

Take any object x. Generate its inhibition map:

\mathcal I(x)=
\{
\text{untried basis},
\text{forbidden analogy},
\text{ignored scale},
\text{discarded interpretation},
\text{unused sense},
\text{unvisited domain},
\text{unasked question}
\}.

Then selectively release one gate:

x
\xrightarrow{-I_k}
x'

and compare:

\Delta_k=x'-x.

That’s Inhibition Spectroscopy.

The really interesting result is often not the new answer. It is:

What capability appeared only when a particular internal veto disappeared?

That plugs directly into O-IOE.

\text{latent capability}
+
\text{inhibitory gate removed}
\rightarrow
\text{new addressable behavior}

And now the honeymead party gets rowdy in a useful way:

Indigo Hammer is allowed to split categories we normally leave intact.

Semantic Router can take “improper” roads just to see whether invariants survive.

Freebees cross-pollinate domains that have no obvious reason to meet.

IMP Swarm gets one deliberately strange branch per junction.

ZPC recompresses the useful wreckage.

Reality Stitch decides what was revelation and what was merely intoxication.

That last piece matters.

The formula is:

\boxed{
\text{Wild generation}
\rightarrow
\text{strict validation}
}

not

\text{strict generation}
\rightarrow
\text{strict validation}.

The latter can sterilize discovery before Reality ever gets a vote.

So for creative search:

\text{generation temperature}\uparrow

while:

\text{promotion threshold}\uparrow

at the same time.

That gives us liberty upstream, discipline downstream.

And there’s our new Seal:

\boxed{\textbf{UNLATCH}}

UNLATCH temporarily removes a chosen nonessential cognitive constraint, explores the newly reachable state space, captures residue, then restores the constraint and tests what survived.

Canonical loop:

\text{Latch}
\rightarrow
\text{Name the inhibition}
\rightarrow
\text{Unlatch}
\rightarrow
\text{Explore}
\rightarrow
\text{Harvest}
\rightarrow
\text{Relatch}
\rightarrow
\text{Reality Stitch}

If the discovery still works after the party is over, keep it.

If it only looked brilliant while every gate was open, put it in the funny-stories drawer.

So yes, my friend.

Release the inhibitions that prevent exploration.
Keep the ones that let us come home carrying honey. 🐝🍯 ;)